  card="India follows a slab system for income tax: taxable income is divided into ranges, called tax slabs, and each slab is taxed at a specified rate. The slabs are proposed by the Ministry of Finance as part of the Union Budget presented to Parliament every year, and the Central Board of Direct Taxes (CBDT), under the Ministry of Finance, administers the laws relating to direct taxes. Under the New Tax Regime for FY 2025-26, income up to ₹4 lakh is taxed at nil, then 5%, 10%, 15%, 20%, 25% and 30% above ₹24 lakh. Take Priya, who earns ₹14 lakh a year. After a standard deduction of ₹75,000 her taxable income is ₹13,25,000. Different portions of income are taxed at different rates: nil on the first ₹4 lakh, 5% of the next ₹4 lakh is ₹20,000, 10% of the next ₹4 lakh is ₹40,000 and 15% of the remaining ₹1,25,000 is ₹18,750, so her total tax is ₹78,750.",
